hi there steve kaufman today i want to  talk about  learning languages via the word of the  day  now there are lots of services that can  send you a word every day or you learn a  word of day and new language  in fact  just today i was at my crossfit session  and there was a fellow there we were  talking about language learning and he  said that he's been on this word of the  day but  he said he has trouble remembering the  word of the day and of course my opinion  is that  you know having this word of the day  program  spit a word out at you every day  there's nothing wrong with it  i mean it's giving you some exposure to  the language it's certainly not a  negative  however  it's extremely inefficient as a way of  learning a language it is really in a  sense next to useless  the reason i say that is and bear in  mind if you like doing that by all means  the number one rule of language learning  is do what you like to do however  uh we need to learn so many words in my  opinion you know we need to learn 10 000  15 000 words uh in most languages  um and even then  a lot of context is beyond our grasp  because we're missing some of the key  words  now at a word a day that's 365 words a  year  except that it's far less than that  because  any list of words  you know if you are able to remember a  quarter of them you're doing very well i  can't i forget most of what i learn most  words that i learn i forget and it's  only because i run into these words  again and again  in my reading and listening  of course a link i've highlighted it now  in yellow and i see it again as a word  that i've encountered before and i  slowly by seeing it in different  contexts  i  learn that word and in fact i learn lots  of words at link because  it's not only the words that i save but  as we know more words we're able to  infer more words so we actually learn  what i call incidentally  more words than we learn deliberately  all from meaningful  content  interesting and meaningful content that  we're reading and listening to  and so a word a day  possibly a hundred words a year  given how quickly we forget what we  learn  maximum i would say a hundred words a  year  um you need ten thousand words  uh that's a lot of years  ten years gets due to  a thousand words  so a hundred years would get you to ten  thousand words so a word a day is  relatively  useless  insofar as languages learning is  concerned in fact any kind of learning  words out of context including in my  opinion  using  spaced repetition systems  is not as effective as massive exposure  to the language through lots of  meaningful listening and reading  otherwise you accumulate this tremendous  list  as they call a deck you know of  flashcards that you spend  if i have an hour a day to spend on  language learning i've got to spend an  hour reviewing my flashcards i don't  have an hour then to spend on listening  and reading so  as you've heard before i think that the  by far the most effective way of  learning a language is to  get through that beginner material  get yourself into interesting authentic  material and spend as much time as  possible listening to it and reading it  and don't be trapped into thinking that  the word a day is doing anything other  than amusing you and before i forget it  reminds me this uh the sort of literacy  uh uh organization in canada  did a deal with a newspaper where they  had this word of the day in english  where they teach people  a new word maybe how to spell it or a  word that would be pushing the  boundaries of their vocabulary one word  a day doesn't do much doesn't do much  not in literacy not in language learning  get into reading and listening whether  it be in a language you're learning or  even in your own language to push the  boundaries of your vocabulary and  comprehension that will give you a base  from which you can start speaking better  thank you for listening bye for now
 

